# WordPress ruleset: vendored upstream (see default.rules for the pinning
# discipline, the DIVERGENCES section below, the project exemptions, and why this
# is duplicated rather than @include'd).
#
# ============ DIVERGENCES FROM UPSTREAM v0.14.0 ============================
#
# 1. ASCII fix, one character. Upstream's comment on the open_basedir rule held a
#    U+2026 horizontal ellipsis ("chdir('..');<ellipsis>;chdir('..')"). The docs
#    say rules, comments included, must be ASCII, or "all rules after non-ASCII
#    symbols [are] not considered for execution and silently discarded"
#    (doc/source/config.rst). Confirmed by bisection: with the ellipsis in place,
#    every rule after that comment (the system/exec/shell_exec/proc_open injection
#    block, the include/require coverage, the backdoor-recon, curl, upload and
#    logging blocks, the webshell patterns) silently stopped registering, with no
#    parse error, no [config] warning and nothing in `docker logs`. Three ASCII
#    periods restore all of them. Applied inline at the `open_basedir` comment.
#
# 2. move_uploaded_file() destination patterns anchored to the real extension.
#    Upstream's `\.ph` / `\.ht` are unanchored and match those letters anywhere in
#    the destination, so `holiday.photo.jpg`, `my.phone.png`, `chart.ht.svg` and
#    `summer.photos.zip` all fatal; WordPress's wp_handle_upload() passes the
#    sanitized user filename here, so such media uploads broke. The patterns are
#    anchored to end-of-path with any run of letters/digits after "ph"/"ht",
#    keeping upstream's breadth (.php, .php3, .phtml, .phar, .pht, .htaccess,
#    .htpasswd, ...), and case-folded because upload names may be uppercase.
#
#    A bare `$` anchor stopped blocking `shell.php ` (trailing space) and
#    `shell.php.` (trailing dot), which the unanchored rule caught by accident.
#    Severity is low (nginx's `\.php$` routing has the same limitation) but it is
#    a regression, so the anchor is `[ .]*$`: any run of trailing spaces and dots
#    is absorbed after the extension, and it cannot reach back into an earlier
#    "ph"/"ht" the way the unanchored pattern did. Trailing tabs are not covered:
#    the config DSL does not reliably pass `\t` through to the regex engine as a
#    tab (a real 0x09 byte did not match a `\t` pattern), and an escape that
#    silently does nothing is worse than none.
#
#    Verified: the four legitimate names above save cleanly; shell.php, .PHP,
#    .phtml, .phar, .htaccess and .htpasswd, multi-character and mixed trailing
#    runs (`shell.php   `, `shell.php...`, `shell.php. `) and `shell.php.php` all
#    still fatal. Applied inline in the "File upload" section.
#
# 3. move_uploaded_file()'s new_path rules are gated >=70000, not upstream's
#    >=70400. PHP 7.2 and 7.3 also name the argument new_path (arginfo: path,
#    new_path), so under upstream's gate they got only "destination" rules, a name
#    no PHP this image builds has, and moved an upload to shell.php unchecked.
#
# 4. Parameter-name gates corrected to PHP's real arginfo. move_uploaded_file() is
#    (path, new_path) on 7.x and (from, to) on 8.x; ini_set() is (varname,
#    newvalue) on 7.x and (option, value) on 8.x. Upstream gates the new_path and
#    "Logging lockdown" varname rules at >=70000 and adds a "destination" branch
#    no PHP version has used, so every move_uploaded_file() call and every
#    ini_set('error_log' | 'error_reporting' | 'display_errors') call on 8.x raised
#    [config] warnings for a nonexistent parameter. These are PHP warnings, not log
#    noise: PrestaShop's webservice turns any warning into an error response, and
#    POST /api/images/products/<id> returned 500 on 7.4 and 8.0. The new_path and
#    varname branches are gated < 80000 and the "destination" branch is dropped;
#    the drops still cover every version under the real argument name. Applied
#    inline in the "File upload" and "Logging lockdown" sections.
# =========== END DIVERGENCES FROM UPSTREAM v0.14.0 =======================
#
# No WordPress-specific delta ships here. A delta rule ships only if it is
# demonstrated both to block a real attack shape and to leave the framework's
# normal operation intact, and these candidates failed that bar, so this file is
# upstream's defaults plus the shared exemptions:
#   - sp.ini_protection.enable() + policy_silent_fail() are inert with no
#     sp.ini.key() entries: src/sp_ini.c hits `if (!entry) return true` (allowed)
#     under the default policy_readwrite when no keys are configured, and a
#     directive that does nothing manufactures false confidence.
#   - create_function() was removed from PHP in 8.0, so a rule is a no-op here.
#   - assert() stopped evaluating string arguments in 8.0, so
#     sp.disable_function.function("assert").drop() has no security value on this
#     image, while zend.assertions=1 here (nothing in conf/*.ini changes it) means
#     native assert() calls execute: assert(1===1), a benign invariant check any
#     dependency might use, fatals under that rule.
# ========= BEGIN project exemptions ==========================================
# Exemptions are evaluated before "BEGIN upstream", so each wins by file position
# over the vendored block's unconditional rule for the same function: rules for a
# function are evaluated top to bottom across the whole file and the first match
# wins (doc/source/config.rst), even with unrelated rules between an early
# .allow() and a later .drop().
#
# Most exist because the vendored rules broke Laravel, WordPress and PrestaShop on
# their first request when booted against real framework source (synthetic probes
# only test what was thought of): ini_set('display_errors', ...) in Laravel's
# exception bootstrap, function_exists('proc_open') in Symfony Console (loaded by
# every `php artisan` and by WordPress/PrestaShop's Symfony Console tooling), and
# eval() in Twig/Smarty template compilation.
#
# Every param-named exemption must be version-split as upstream splits its own
# (@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000 / >= 80000): snuffleupagus matches .param() by
# argument name and PHP 8.0 renamed several (ini_set: varname -> option; putenv:
# setting -> assignment). Snuffleupagus ships here for php >= 7.2, so an exemption
# using only the 8.x name never matches on 7.x while the vendored drop rules, which
# upstream did split, still fire there.
#
# 1. putenv('DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH'): Composer's Platform::clearEnv() calls it during
#    git version-guessing (composer/src/Composer/Util/Git.php, "clean up env for
#    OSX"), and Symfony components do the same defensive clear. Upstream blocks any
#    putenv assignment containing the substring "LD_" to stop LD_PRELOAD and
#    LD_LIBRARY_PATH injection; "DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH" matches but is macOS-only and
#    inert on this Linux image, so blocking it protects nothing. Exempted by exact
#    value, not pattern: LD_PRELOAD, LD_LIBRARY_PATH and every other real LD_
#    vector still match the substring rule (verified both directions, and that the
#    test has teeth: a catch-all exemption wrongly allowed LD_PRELOAD).
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value("DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH").allow();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value("DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH").allow();
@end_condition;

# 2. ini_set('display_errors' | 'memory_limit', ...), both read from framework
#    source: Laravel's HandleExceptions.php calls ini_set('display_errors', 'Off')
#    on every non-testing request; WordPress's wp_debug_mode()
#    (wp-includes/load.php, every bootstrap) and wp_raise_memory_limit() (every
#    wp-admin page) call ini_set() on display_errors and memory_limit;
#    PrestaShop's config/defines.inc.php calls ini_set('display_errors', ...) on
#    every request depending on _PS_MODE_DEV_, and its SymfonyCacheClearer admin
#    operation calls ini_set('memory_limit', '-1').
#
#    max_execution_time is not exempted: upstream's ini_set drop list is
#    assert.active/zend.assertions/memory_limit/include_path/open_basedir plus
#    error_log/error_reporting/display_errors, so nothing blocks it.
#
#    These are full-key exemptions (any value), not value-scoped like the putenv
#    one, because the rule grammar allows at most one param/pos selector and one
#    value/value_r matcher per rule ("duplicate keyword 'param'", "duplicate
#    keyword 'value_r'", "'.value' and '.value_r' are mutually exclusive"). Upstream
#    says the same of open_basedir: "Since we have no way of matching on two
#    parameters at the same time, we're blocking calls to open_basedir altogether".
#    A rule scoped to the option name necessarily allows every value for it.
#
#    For display_errors that means the ON direction, the information-disclosure
#    risk the vendored rule guards against, is not blocked either
#    (ini_set("display_errors","1") succeeds). That is acceptable: the
#    realistic path to attacker-controlled display_errors=on is a narrow
#    options-injection bug, not general RCE, and anyone with arbitrary code
#    execution has better exfiltration paths. error_log and error_reporting, which
#    would let an attacker redirect or silence logging, stay blocked.
#
#    For memory_limit, the DoS the upstream rule guards against is not bounded by
#    anything else: a container hitting its cgroup limit is OOM-killed whole,
#    taking every FPM worker with it, which is worse than the per-request fatal
#    the rule produced. The exemption stays because WordPress's admin dashboard
#    needs it on every page load and an attacker-controlled memory_limit needs the
#    same options-injection precondition as display_errors. It protects little on
#    its own merits, and nothing else catches the worst case.
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("display_errors").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("memory_limit").allow();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("display_errors").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("memory_limit").allow();
@end_condition;

# Not exempted, deliberately: disable_functions, allow_url_include, extension_dir
# and auto_prepend_file have no ini_set rule anywhere in this file and need none.
# They are PHP_INI_SYSTEM/PHP_INI_PERDIR settings that PHP itself refuses to change
# at runtime (ini_set("disable_functions", "") returns false). That protection
# comes from PHP's ini scoping, not from this ruleset.

# 3. function_exists() / is_callable() are removed from the recon block outright,
#    not narrowed: introspection is not invocation. Upstream treats a
#    function_exists()/is_callable() check on eval, exec, system, shell_exec,
#    proc_open or passthru as suspicious, but that is what defensive code does
#    before conditionally using them: Symfony Console's Terminal class (loaded by
#    every `php artisan` command and by WordPress/PrestaShop's Symfony Console
#    tooling) calls function_exists('proc_open')/is_callable('proc_open') before
#    shelling out for terminal width. The capability check adds no protection the
#    block on the calls themselves does not already give; system(), exec() and
#    proc_open() calls stay covered by the command-injection rules.
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").allow();
#
# There is no `require` exemption for Composer's phar bootstrap
# (`/usr/local/bin/composer`): even one scoped to that root-owned calling file would
# not make Composer work, because past that call it reaches
# ini_set('display_errors') and function_exists/is_callable('proc_open') (exempted
# above for framework compatibility generally; setting COLUMNS/LINES skips the
# Terminal probe anyway), and finally proc_open() itself, where piped commands
# match the command-injection filter this ruleset exists to provide
# (`proc_open("echo hi")` is allowed, `proc_open("ls | grep x")` is not; item 8
# exempts only Symfony Console's terminal-width probe). That is the headline
# protection working, not a false positive like the putenv case, and Composer's
# git, VCS and plugin-script work would likely surface more.
# So this is a documented property, not a bug: rulesets serve runtime request
# handling, Composer is build-time tooling, and the intended pattern is to build
# with `cli-builder` and no ruleset and serve with `fpm`/`cli` and one.
# `PHP_SNUFFLEUPAGUS` and Composer are not meant to run together; do not "fix"
# this by exempting proc_open's metacharacter class.

# 4. curl_setopt($h, CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER, true). Without this exemption
#    wordpress.rules dropped the call, which breaks every outbound HTTP request of
#    WordPress core (WP_Http_Curl::request(), used by the update checks on
#    ordinary wp-admin loads). Upstream's allow rules below
#    (`.param("value").value("1")` / `.value("2")`) match the strings "1"/"2", but
#    WP_Http_Curl passes the PHP bool true, which matches neither, so the call
#    falls through to the `option == 64` drop. That drop exists so VERIFYPEER can
#    never be turned off; enabling verification (curl's default) is the secure
#    direction, so blocking it is a pure false positive. "true" is added as a safe
#    value ahead of the vendored drop, the allow-before-drop shape upstream uses.
#    "false" is deliberately not added: curl_setopt($h, CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER,
#    false) still fatals, the one direction that must stay blocked.
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value_r("^(1|true)$").allow();

# 5. ini_get() is removed from the recon block outright, as in 3: reading a
#    setting is not changing it. Upstream drops ini_get() of allow_url_fopen,
#    open_basedir and suhosin.*, each an ordinary capability check in the
#    applications served: PrestaShop 8's AdminController reads allow_url_fopen on
#    every back-office page (the whole back office fataled), proxy-manager-lts
#    reads suhosin.executor.disable_eval before generating a proxy, Guzzle reads
#    allow_url_fopen to pick a handler, and WordPress's automatic updater and site
#    health read open_basedir. Changing them stays covered: open_basedir by the
#    ini_set() drops below; allow_url_fopen and suhosin.* are INI_SYSTEM and cannot
#    be changed at runtime.
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").allow();

# 6. include of a non-.php path from framework files that do it by design. Scoped
#    by the calling file (.filename_r()), not the include target, so an include
#    anywhere else still has to pass the extension allow-list below. Shared by all
#    four rulesets; a path not in the app being served never matches.
#    - Symfony's HtmlErrorRenderer inlines its CSS/JS/SVG assets with include;
#      without this every error page Symfony renders (a 405 in Laravel, any
#      Symfony error page in PrestaShop) became a 500.
#    - Symfony's PHP-files cache (PhpFilesTrait in 3.4/4.4, PhpFilesAdapter in 5+)
#      includes its entries, which are written without an extension; PrestaShop's
#      back office fataled on its first cache read.
#    - laravel/serializable-closure rebuilds a closure by including its own
#      laravel-serializable-closure:// stream. `route:cache` stores closure routes
#      that way, so with a cached route table Laravel 11+'s /up health route and
#      every other closure route or queued closure fataled.
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/(symfony/error-handler|Symfony/Component/ErrorHandler)/ErrorRenderer/HtmlErrorRenderer\\.php$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/Symfony/Component/Cache/Traits/PhpFilesTrait\\.php$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/symfony/cache/Adapter/PhpFilesAdapter\\.php$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/laravel/serializable-closure/src/Serializers/Native\\.php$").allow();

# 7. curl_setopt(CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST | CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER, 0) from
#    WordPress core's HTTP transport. WordPress turns verification off only when
#    the caller passed sslverify=false, and core does so for its loopback requests
#    to itself: the site health checks (a 500 on /wp-admin/site-health.php and its
#    REST endpoint) and spawn_cron(), which runs on an ordinary page view whenever
#    an event is due, so with the stock WP-Cron setup a visitor's request fataled.
#    Scoped to that one calling file and those two options; a direct curl_setopt()
#    anywhere else still cannot turn verification off. Code that asks WordPress for
#    sslverify=false gets it, as it does without this ruleset.
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").filename_r("/wp-includes/Requests/src/Transport/Curl\\.php$").param("option").value("81").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").filename_r("/wp-includes/Requests/src/Transport/Curl\\.php$").param("option").value("64").allow();

# 8. proc_open('stty -a | grep columns') from Symfony Console's Terminal class:
#    that one command, byte for byte, from that one file. Symfony up to 5.x
#    measures the terminal this way whenever COLUMNS is unset, and a web request
#    gets there too: Laravel's Artisan::call(), PrestaShop 8's back-office "clear
#    cache" (which runs cache:clear in-process, so the drop landed after the
#    redirect and the cache silently never cleared). The metacharacter filter is
#    not widened and any other piped command stays blocked; this is an exact value.
#    These images are public, so a ruleset has to run the applications it is named
#    for unmodified, without asking users to set COLUMNS/LINES or patch vendor
#    code. Symfony 6+ passes an argv array (['stty', '-a']), which the string
#    filter never matched.
sp.disable_function.function("proc_open").filename_r("/(symfony/console|Symfony/Component/Console)/Terminal\\.php$").param("command").value("stty -a | grep columns").allow();
# ========== END project exemptions ===========================================
#
# ============================ BEGIN upstream ============================
# This is the default configuration file for Snuffleupagus (https://snuffleupagus.rtfd.io).
# It contains "reasonable" defaults that won't break your websites,
# and a lot of commented directives that you can enable if you want to 
# have a better protection.

# Harden the PRNG
sp.harden_random.enable();

# sp.xxe_protection.enable() is deliberately absent: it switches libxml's entity
# loader off from an INI handler at startup, which does not carry into requests,
# so LIBXML_NOENT still expands file:// entities with it on. On PHP 7 it also
# nops the application's own libxml_disable_entity_loader(true). It would claim
# protection it does not give.

# Global configuration variables
# sp.global.secret_key("YOU _DO_ NEED TO CHANGE THIS WITH SOME RANDOM CHARACTERS.");

# Globally activate strict mode
# https://www.php.net/manual/en/language.types.declarations.php#language.types.declarations.strict
# sp.global_strict.enable();

# Prevent unserialize-related exploits
# sp.unserialize_hmac.enable();

# Only allow execution of read-only files. This is a low-hanging fruit that you should enable.
# sp.readonly_exec.enable();

# PHP has a lot of wrappers, most of them aren't usually useful, you should
# only enable the ones you're using.
# sp.wrappers_whitelist.list("file,php,phar");

# Prevent sloppy comparisons.
# sp.sloppy_comparison.enable();

# Use SameSite on session cookie
# https://snuffleupagus.readthedocs.io/features.html#protection-against-cross-site-request-forgery
sp.cookie.name("PHPSESSID").samesite("lax");

# Note that an attacker with arbitrary PHP code execution
# can bypass some virtual-patching, by (as)using PHP feature.
# A clever example would be to declare a class with a __toString method.

# Harden the `chmod` function (0777 (oct = 511, 0666 = 438)
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("mode").value("438").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("mode").value("511").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("permissions").value("438").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("permissions").value("511").drop();
@end_condition;

# Prevent various `mail`-related vulnerabilities
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("mail").param("additional_parameters").value_r("\\-").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("mb_send_mail").param("additional_parameters").value_r("\\-").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("mail").param("additional_params").value_r("\\-").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("mb_send_mail").param("additional_params").value_r("\\-").drop();
@end_condition;

# Since it's now burned, me might as well mitigate it publicly
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value_r("LD_").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value("PATH").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value_r("LD_").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value("PATH").drop();
@end_condition;

# This one was burned in Nov 2019 - https://gist.github.com/LoadLow/90b60bd5535d6c3927bb24d5f9955b80
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value_r("GCONV_").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value_r("GCONV_").drop()
@end_condition;

# https://github.com/php/php-src/issues/22035
# CURLOPT_SSLENGINE = 10089 
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("10089").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("10089").drop()
@end_condition;

# Since people are stupid enough to use `extract` on things like $_GET or $_POST, we might as well mitigate this vector
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("extract").pos("0").value_r("^_").drop()
sp.disable_function.function("extract").pos("1").value("0").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("extract").param("array").value_r("^_").drop()
sp.disable_function.function("extract").param("flags").value("0").drop()
@end_condition;

# This is also burned:
# ini_set('open_basedir','..');chdir('..');...;chdir('..');ini_set('open_basedir','/');echo(file_get_contents('/etc/passwd'));
# Since we have no way of matching on two parameters at the same time, we're
# blocking calls to open_basedir altogether: nobody is using it via ini_set anyway.
# Moreover, there are non-public bypasses that are also using this vector ;)
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("open_basedir").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("open_basedir").drop()
@end_condition;

# Prevent various `include`-related vulnerabilities
sp.disable_function.function("require_once").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include_once").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("require").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("require_once").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("include_once").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("require").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("include").drop();

# Prevent `system`-related injections
sp.disable_function.function("system").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("shell_exec").pos("0").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("shell_exec").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
@end_condition;
sp.disable_function.function("exec").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("proc_open").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();

# Prevent runtime modification of interesting things
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("assert.active").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("zend.assertions").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("memory_limit").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("include_path").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("open_basedir").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("assert.active").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("zend.assertions").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("memory_limit").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("include_path").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("open_basedir").drop();
@end_condition;

# Detect some backdoors via environment recon
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("varname").value("allow_url_fopen").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("varname").value("open_basedir").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("varname").value_r("suhosin").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("passthru").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("passthru").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("option").value("allow_url_fopen").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("option").value("open_basedir").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("option").value_r("suhosin").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("passthru").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("passthru").drop();
@end_condition;


# Ghetto error-based sqli detection
# sp.disable_function.function("mysql_query").ret("FALSE").drop();
# sp.disable_function.function("mysqli_query").ret("FALSE").drop();
# sp.disable_function.function("PDO::query").ret("FALSE").drop();

# Ensure that certificates are properly verified
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value("1").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value("2").allow();
# `81` is SSL_VERIFYHOST and `64` SSL_VERIFYPEER
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("64").drop().alias("Please don't turn CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYCLIENT off.");
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("81").drop().alias("Please don't turn CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST off.");

# Ensure that file:// protocol is not allowed in CURL
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value_r("file://").drop().alias("file:// protocol is disabled");
sp.disable_function.function("curl_init").param("url").value_r("file://").drop().alias("file:// protocol is disabled");

# File upload
#
# Local correction (DIVERGENCES item 2): upstream's "\.ph" / "\.ht" patterns are
# unanchored and match those letters anywhere in the destination, so
# holiday.photo.jpg, my.phone.png, chart.ht.svg and summer.photos.zip all fatal,
# and wp_handle_upload() passes the sanitized user filename here. The patterns are
# anchored to end-of-path with any run of letters/digits after "ph"/"ht", keeping
# upstream's breadth (.php, .php3, .phtml, .phar, .pht, .htaccess, .htpasswd, ...),
# and case-folded ([pP][hH], [hH][tT]) since upload names may be uppercase.
#
# The anchor is `[ .]*$`, not a bare `$`, which would stop blocking `shell.php `
# and `shell.php.` (the unanchored rule caught them by accident). Any run of
# trailing spaces and/or dots is absorbed before the end anchor; trailing tabs are
# not, because the config DSL does not reliably pass `\t` through to the regex
# engine as a tab.
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("to").value_r("\\.[pP][hH][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("to").value_r("\\.[hH][tT][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("new_path").value_r("\\.[pP][hH][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("new_path").value_r("\\.[hH][tT][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
@end_condition;

# Logging lockdown
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("error_log").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("error_reporting").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("display_errors").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("error_log").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("error_reporting").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("display_errors").drop();
@end_condition;

# Classic webshells patterns
sp.disable_function.function("system>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("shell_exec>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("exec>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("passthru>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("proc_open>base64_decode").drop();
sp.eval_blacklist.list("system,exec,shell_exec,passthru,proc_open");
# ============================= END upstream ==============================

# ============================ our addition ================================
# Rejects uploads whose content is executable PHP, regardless of extension, via the
# sp-upload-check script this project ships (rootfs/usr/local/bin/sp-upload-check);
# upstream's default.rules has no equivalent. It skips archives by magic byte
# (WordPress plugin and PrestaShop module installs upload zips by design, and a
# naive content sniff rejected them depending on how zip stored their entries),
# catches the `<?=` short-echo tag alongside `<?php`, and fails closed (rejects)
# when it cannot read the file. See that script's header.
sp.upload_validation.script("/usr/local/bin/sp-upload-check").enable();
